1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,600
You own your phone.

2
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:04,150
You paid for it. It's yours.

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,370
It's in your pocket right now.

4
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:09,860
And Apple decides what
is allowed to run on it.

5
00:00:10,830 --> 00:00:12,780
Not just software that's dangerous.

6
00:00:13,470 --> 00:00:15,120
Not just software that's broken.

7
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:18,440
Software Apple hasn't put its stamp on.

8
00:00:19,330 --> 00:00:23,520
That is not a complaint about
quality. The phone is superb.

9
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:27,010
It's a question about
who the thing belongs to.

10
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:32,500
And once you notice it, you
start seeing it everywhere.

11
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:37,260
And it's why some people
can't stand this company.

12
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:43,670
In 2016, the iPhone 7 arrived
without a headphone socket.

13
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:48,520
The plug it dropped had been
standard since the 1960s.

14
00:00:49,115 --> 00:00:50,855
Stereos. Aeroplane armrests.

15
00:00:50,935 --> 00:00:52,765
Almost every phone in the shop.

16
00:00:53,775 --> 00:00:56,125
Apple went on stage and gave a reason.

17
00:00:57,205 --> 00:00:58,643
Courage.

18
00:01:00,493 --> 00:01:03,063
That isn't a reason. It's a slogan.

19
00:01:04,043 --> 00:01:05,733
And Apple was right anyway.

20
00:01:06,612 --> 00:01:09,392
Three months before that
phone went on sale, wireless

21
00:01:09,472 --> 00:01:12,982
headphones had already passed
wired ones in American shops.

22
00:01:14,232 --> 00:01:15,662
Not by how many sold.

23
00:01:15,772 --> 00:01:18,182
By how much people spent on them.

24
00:01:20,692 --> 00:01:22,692
Being told what you need,
and then discovering

25
00:01:22,772 --> 00:01:25,482
you did need it, is a
very particular feeling.

26
00:01:28,532 --> 00:01:30,222
And it wasn't the only port.

27
00:01:31,012 --> 00:01:33,772
In 2012, Apple replaced
the connector on the

28
00:01:33,852 --> 00:01:36,502
bottom of the iPhone with
one of its own design.

29
00:01:37,652 --> 00:01:39,362
It kept it for eleven years.

30
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:44,182
Meanwhile the rest of the industry
settled on a single shared plug.

31
00:01:45,302 --> 00:01:49,172
Apple switched in 2023,
with the iPhone 15.

32
00:01:49,921 --> 00:01:53,551
The year after the European
Union passed a law requiring it.

33
00:01:56,181 --> 00:01:57,851
Now take something breaking.

34
00:01:58,891 --> 00:02:03,641
Your screen cracks. You find the
identical part from an identical phone.

35
00:02:04,701 --> 00:02:07,491
You fit it. It works.
Every pixel lights up.

36
00:02:08,461 --> 00:02:11,171
And your phone tells you
it doesn't recognise it.

37
00:02:12,021 --> 00:02:14,321
The screen, the battery,
the camera and the Face

38
00:02:14,401 --> 00:02:17,571
ID module each carry
their own serial number.

39
00:02:18,781 --> 00:02:21,791
That number is married to your
logic board at the factory.

40
00:02:23,041 --> 00:02:24,611
Swap the part, and the phone knows.

41
00:02:25,951 --> 00:02:30,301
On the iPhone 13, changing a cracked
screen stopped Face ID working.

42
00:02:31,211 --> 00:02:33,021
Not the screen. Face ID.

43
00:02:35,681 --> 00:02:38,131
Fine, you think. Then
I'll do it Apple's way.

44
00:02:38,891 --> 00:02:42,981
Apple does run a programme that sends you
the official parts and the official tools.

45
00:02:44,231 --> 00:02:46,861
A journalist ordered it
to change one battery.

46
00:02:48,041 --> 00:02:52,111
It arrived as two flight cases
weighing about thirty-six kilos.

47
00:02:53,101 --> 00:02:55,311
The battery weighs about thirty grams.

48
00:02:58,311 --> 00:03:00,201
Apple has since loosened this.

49
00:03:00,771 --> 00:03:03,001
Genuine used parts can now be paired.

50
00:03:04,021 --> 00:03:05,671
Which is the interesting bit.

51
00:03:06,441 --> 00:03:07,971
Not that it never changes.

52
00:03:08,521 --> 00:03:10,791
That it changes when somebody pushes.

53
00:03:13,551 --> 00:03:15,381
Then there's the computer itself.

54
00:03:16,541 --> 00:03:21,931
For decades, owning a computer meant you
could open it. Add memory. Change a drive.

55
00:03:22,341 --> 00:03:24,151
Fit a better graphics card.

56
00:03:24,873 --> 00:03:27,463
On a modern Mac, the
memory is not a component.

57
00:03:28,613 --> 00:03:30,723
It is packaged together
with the processor.

58
00:03:31,933 --> 00:03:34,163
There is no slot. There is no stick.

59
00:03:34,713 --> 00:03:36,123
There is nothing to open.

60
00:03:36,933 --> 00:03:38,853
Which means the only
moment you get to decide

61
00:03:38,933 --> 00:03:41,783
how much memory your
computer will ever have...

62
00:03:43,453 --> 00:03:44,783
...is before you buy it.

63
00:03:47,063 --> 00:03:48,733
And it isn't only the hardware.

64
00:03:49,873 --> 00:03:52,683
On the Mac, you can still
install whatever you like.

65
00:03:53,733 --> 00:03:56,103
On the phone, it's the
door we started with.

66
00:03:58,223 --> 00:04:00,493
A developer writes an iPhone app.

67
00:04:01,183 --> 00:04:04,073
Apple decides whether it
reaches anybody at all,

68
00:04:04,163 --> 00:04:06,895
and the standard commission
is thirty per cent.

69
00:04:07,345 --> 00:04:10,895
So: you can't choose the shop.
You can't choose the parts.

70
00:04:11,485 --> 00:04:12,795
You can't choose the memory.

71
00:04:13,645 --> 00:04:14,975
And all of it is yours.

72
00:04:17,955 --> 00:04:20,085
Which brings us to the strangest one.

73
00:04:20,995 --> 00:04:23,815
In 2016, people began noticing that their

74
00:04:23,895 --> 00:04:26,525
older iPhones were
switching themselves off.

75
00:04:27,635 --> 00:04:29,085
Not running out of charge.

76
00:04:29,215 --> 00:04:31,685
Switching off, with charge to spare.

77
00:04:32,845 --> 00:04:34,655
There was a real reason for it.

78
00:04:35,305 --> 00:04:39,035
As a battery ages, it struggles to
deliver a sudden burst of power.

79
00:04:40,235 --> 00:04:43,005
So the phone would cut out in
the middle of doing something.

80
00:04:44,145 --> 00:04:47,955
Apple's fix was to slow the processor
down before that could happen.

81
00:04:49,105 --> 00:04:51,875
Which is, honestly, a
sensible piece of engineering.

82
00:04:53,245 --> 00:04:55,475
Apple shipped it in a software update.

83
00:04:56,555 --> 00:04:58,685
And didn't clearly say
it slowed the phone.

84
00:05:01,365 --> 00:05:05,455
For almost a year, millions of people had
a phone that had been deliberately slowed.

85
00:05:06,485 --> 00:05:08,375
The notes mentioned power management.

86
00:05:09,575 --> 00:05:12,745
It came out because somebody ran
the numbers and published them.

87
00:05:13,835 --> 00:05:17,675
Apple confirmed it, apologised,
and for one year dropped the price

88
00:05:17,755 --> 00:05:21,165
of a replacement battery from
seventy-nine dollars to twenty-nine.

89
00:05:22,401 --> 00:05:24,371
The engineering was defensible.

90
00:05:25,361 --> 00:05:27,091
The communication wasn't.

91
00:05:29,921 --> 00:05:32,771
Now here's the part that
makes this genuinely hard.

92
00:05:33,811 --> 00:05:36,301
Apple isn't doing any
of this to annoy you.

93
00:05:37,221 --> 00:05:41,571
Think about what actually happens when
you take an Apple device out of its box.

94
00:05:42,581 --> 00:05:45,171
Your headphones connect
before you've asked them to.

95
00:05:46,141 --> 00:05:48,351
Your photos are already on your laptop.

96
00:05:49,431 --> 00:05:52,481
You copy something on one
machine and paste it on another.

97
00:05:53,571 --> 00:05:55,501
A phone call rings on your watch.

98
00:05:56,581 --> 00:05:58,031
None of that is magic.

99
00:05:58,871 --> 00:06:02,421
It works because Apple owns both ends
of every one of those connections.

100
00:06:05,411 --> 00:06:08,301
The same control that stops you
fitting a screen you bought...

101
00:06:09,721 --> 00:06:11,691
...is why the headphones just work.

102
00:06:14,631 --> 00:06:16,701
Take one away and you take the other.

103
00:06:17,641 --> 00:06:20,451
And this is why arguing
about Apple goes nowhere.

104
00:06:21,601 --> 00:06:24,811
Because the complaint and the
compliment are about the same thing.

105
00:06:27,741 --> 00:06:30,571
There's one more layer, and
it isn't technical at all.

106
00:06:31,701 --> 00:06:33,611
Apple doesn't just sell a device.

107
00:06:34,441 --> 00:06:36,531
It sells a way of
thinking about technology.

108
00:06:37,791 --> 00:06:39,421
Not: here is a telephone.

109
00:06:40,331 --> 00:06:44,741
But: this changes everything. This
is the future. Think different.

110
00:06:47,013 --> 00:06:48,883
So people don't just own the thing.

111
00:06:49,313 --> 00:06:50,663
They tell you about it.

112
00:06:51,473 --> 00:06:54,223
Some critics say it behaves
almost like a religion.

113
00:06:55,483 --> 00:06:57,833
Not literally. There
are no Apple churches.

114
00:06:58,773 --> 00:07:00,163
But look at the shape of it.

115
00:07:00,933 --> 00:07:02,603
A founder with a legend attached.

116
00:07:03,453 --> 00:07:07,143
Events that happen every
year. Believers. Sceptics.

117
00:07:07,353 --> 00:07:08,883
Arguments that never end.

118
00:07:11,123 --> 00:07:14,233
Very few people describe their
washing machine that way.

119
00:07:15,383 --> 00:07:17,613
And that's where the real conflict starts.

120
00:07:18,643 --> 00:07:21,263
Because "think different"
comes from a company that is

121
00:07:21,343 --> 00:07:24,533
extremely specific about how
you may use its products.

122
00:07:25,773 --> 00:07:27,363
Which sounds like a contradiction.

123
00:07:28,483 --> 00:07:29,683
It isn't.

124
00:07:31,911 --> 00:07:33,801
Apple isn't selling you freedom.

125
00:07:34,771 --> 00:07:37,961
It's selling you a room where you
never have to worry about anything.

126
00:07:39,131 --> 00:07:41,021
Everything in it is placed well.

127
00:07:41,451 --> 00:07:43,091
Nothing is in your way.

128
00:07:43,171 --> 00:07:44,721
Nothing needs configuring.

129
00:07:45,641 --> 00:07:48,791
And for millions of people,
that is exactly what they want.

130
00:07:49,981 --> 00:07:51,431
You can move the furniture.

131
00:07:52,491 --> 00:07:54,721
But if you want to move the walls...

132
00:07:55,491 --> 00:07:56,691
...you can't.

133
00:07:59,530 --> 00:08:03,280
And Apple won't call that a
wall. It'll call it design.

134
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:05,950
You heard it earlier. Courage.

135
00:08:07,276 --> 00:08:11,086
So if it bothers you, there's nothing
to report. There's only a preference.

136
00:08:13,766 --> 00:08:16,296
Imagine two people
buying the same computer.

137
00:08:17,506 --> 00:08:20,516
The first one says: I don't
want to configure anything.

138
00:08:21,146 --> 00:08:24,176
I don't want to spend Saturday
fixing my own machine.

139
00:08:24,866 --> 00:08:26,536
I just want it to work.

140
00:08:27,316 --> 00:08:29,306
For that person, the room is a gift.

141
00:08:30,426 --> 00:08:33,456
The second one says: I want
to choose my own parts.

142
00:08:33,586 --> 00:08:35,396
I want to install what I like.

143
00:08:35,926 --> 00:08:37,656
I want to mend what I bought.

144
00:08:38,481 --> 00:08:40,471
For that person, the room is a cage.

145
00:08:43,211 --> 00:08:44,821
And neither of them is wrong.

146
00:08:45,431 --> 00:08:47,021
They simply want different things.

147
00:08:47,941 --> 00:08:49,521
By the way, if you'd like to see where the

148
00:08:49,601 --> 00:08:52,351
next question takes us,
consider subscribing.

149
00:08:53,201 --> 00:08:54,671
It really helps the channel.

150
00:08:55,661 --> 00:08:59,891
So what is this argument actually
about? Not Apple against Android.

151
00:09:00,681 --> 00:09:02,071
Not Mac against Windows.

152
00:09:03,171 --> 00:09:05,241
It's convenience against control.

153
00:09:08,481 --> 00:09:10,531
Do you want to know how everything works?

154
00:09:10,661 --> 00:09:12,931
Or would you rather never have to?

155
00:09:13,671 --> 00:09:16,581
Apple is extremely good at
offering you the second one.

156
00:09:17,631 --> 00:09:19,971
And that is exactly why
millions of people love

157
00:09:20,051 --> 00:09:23,381
it, and exactly why some
people can't stand it.

158
00:09:25,931 --> 00:09:27,801
Apple doesn't just sell technology.

159
00:09:28,711 --> 00:09:31,481
It sells an idea about
what technology should be.

160
00:09:32,541 --> 00:09:35,031
And whether you love
that idea or hate it...

161
00:09:36,671 --> 00:09:39,761
...Apple has spent decades
making sure you know what it is.
